1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,280
You've woken up with Star Platinum.

2
00:00:02,280 --> 00:00:05,260
Jotaro's Stand, from JoJo's Bizarre Adventure.

3
00:00:06,020 --> 00:00:06,780
In real life.

4
00:00:07,380 --> 00:00:10,600
It's faster than anything you've ever seen, and

5
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:13,220
in forty days it will stop time for

6
00:00:13,220 --> 00:00:13,780
one breath.

7
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:17,820
But this morning, it's just seven twelve.

8
00:00:17,820 --> 00:00:19,680
A glass slides off your nightstand.

9
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:21,380
You don't reach for it.

10
00:00:22,180 --> 00:00:23,280
Something else does.

11
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:27,040
A huge arm, blue and purple, comes out

12
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:29,280
of your shoulder and puts the glass back

13
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:31,080
before your own hand has even moved.

14
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:34,960
And before tonight, it's going to cost you

15
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:36,380
something you can't replace.

16
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:38,260
Nobody else can see it.

17
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:40,940
Your mum walks in and asks why you're

18
00:00:40,940 --> 00:00:42,020
staring at the wardrobe.

19
00:00:42,940 --> 00:00:45,000
In the story this is called a Stand,

20
00:00:45,420 --> 00:00:47,460
and Star Platinum's base powers are simple:

21
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:51,600
strength, speed and precision, all far beyond a

22
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:51,820
human.

23
00:00:52,420 --> 00:00:54,580
You think "left" and it's already moved.

24
00:00:55,440 --> 00:00:58,000
Lesson one: you're not the strong one.

25
00:00:58,480 --> 00:00:59,840
Something next to you is.

26
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,540
At noon you go out for bread, because

27
00:01:03,540 --> 00:01:04,800
you're trying to act normal.

28
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:08,500
A kid's trolley clips a tower of tinned

29
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:10,720
tomatoes, two metres tall, and it starts to

30
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:10,940
fall.

31
00:01:11,620 --> 00:01:14,860
You flinch, and Star Platinum catches all of

32
00:01:14,860 --> 00:01:15,220
it.

33
00:01:15,220 --> 00:01:18,200
Forty tins, back on the shelf, labels out.

34
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:20,160
Then it keeps going.

35
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:22,160
It straightens the shelf next to it.

36
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:23,780
Then the pasta display.

37
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:27,360
The manager watches forty tins fly back into

38
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:30,520
place all by themselves, and you leave without

39
00:01:30,520 --> 00:01:31,080
any bread.

40
00:01:32,140 --> 00:01:34,660
Lesson two: it doesn't know when to stop.

41
00:01:35,140 --> 00:01:36,560
You have to tell it.

42
00:01:37,260 --> 00:01:39,320
That evening you take it to an empty

43
00:01:39,320 --> 00:01:39,580
gym.

44
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:42,600
One of the heaviest deadlifts ever recorded is

45
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:45,020
about five hundred and ten kilograms, lifted by

46
00:01:45,020 --> 00:01:47,520
Hafthor Bjornsson in twenty twenty-five.

47
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:48,880
A human at the very edge.

48
00:01:48,880 --> 00:01:51,500
You load every plate in the building onto

49
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:52,100
one bar.

50
00:01:52,920 --> 00:01:54,660
Star Platinum picks it up with one hand,

51
00:01:54,660 --> 00:01:55,800
like a shopping bag.

52
00:01:56,260 --> 00:01:57,940
The bar bends in the middle like a

53
00:01:57,940 --> 00:01:58,340
banana.

54
00:01:59,080 --> 00:02:01,280
The owner walks in, looks at the bar,

55
00:02:01,660 --> 00:02:04,260
looks at you, and cancels your membership on

56
00:02:04,260 --> 00:02:04,680
the spot.

57
00:02:05,640 --> 00:02:06,040
Lesson three:

58
00:02:06,720 --> 00:02:07,420
strength is easy.

59
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:09,940
Putting things down gently is the hard part.

60
00:02:11,060 --> 00:02:13,080
You get home and your hands are shaking,

61
00:02:13,080 --> 00:02:14,360
and you notice something:

62
00:02:15,140 --> 00:02:17,700
everything it does, you feel afterwards.

63
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:21,020
Every fan knows what comes next.

64
00:02:21,020 --> 00:02:23,480
ORA ORA ORA.

65
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:24,960
So you try it on a heavy bag.

66
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:28,100
Real boxers' punches move at roughly six to

67
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:29,360
eleven metres per second.

68
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:31,940
Now say, just as an example, that a

69
00:02:31,940 --> 00:02:34,480
Stand throws twenty punches a second at that

70
00:02:34,480 --> 00:02:34,800
speed.

71
00:02:35,540 --> 00:02:37,460
The bag lasts four seconds.

72
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:38,580
Then it splits,

73
00:02:39,220 --> 00:02:41,240
and sand pours across the floor like an

74
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:41,740
hourglass.

75
00:02:42,580 --> 00:02:43,580
You sit down in it.

76
00:02:44,040 --> 00:02:44,680
You're not hungry.

77
00:02:45,260 --> 00:02:45,980
You're starving.

78
00:02:46,660 --> 00:02:47,980
You eat three dinners that night.

79
00:02:48,520 --> 00:02:49,980
You've started calling it Gary.

80
00:02:50,860 --> 00:02:51,900
It doesn't answer to Gary.

81
00:02:52,700 --> 00:02:53,100
Lesson four:

82
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:55,800
every punch has to be paid for, and

83
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:56,680
you're the one who pays.

84
00:02:58,140 --> 00:03:00,580
At night your keys fall through a drain

85
00:03:00,580 --> 00:03:00,840
grate.

86
00:03:01,760 --> 00:03:03,900
In the story, Star Platinum has a move

87
00:03:03,900 --> 00:03:06,780
called Star Finger: it stretches two of its

88
00:03:06,780 --> 00:03:08,960
fingers to reach further than its arms can.

89
00:03:10,040 --> 00:03:12,460
Two fingers slide down between the bars like

90
00:03:12,460 --> 00:03:14,880
chopsticks, and come back up with your keys,

91
00:03:15,680 --> 00:03:18,800
a two-euro coin, and somebody's wedding ring.

92
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:20,780
You hand the ring in at the police

93
00:03:20,780 --> 00:03:21,200
station.

94
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:23,820
You don't explain how you got it.

95
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:27,320
Lesson five: the useful tricks are the small

96
00:03:27,320 --> 00:03:27,680
ones.

97
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:31,120
A boy in the park films you catching

98
00:03:31,120 --> 00:03:32,860
his football before it hits a pram.

99
00:03:33,380 --> 00:03:35,620
In the video you're standing still, and the

100
00:03:35,620 --> 00:03:38,280
ball just stops in the air, a metre

101
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:38,880
from the baby.

102
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:41,020
Some people say it's edited.

103
00:03:41,440 --> 00:03:42,740
Some people say it's a ghost.

104
00:03:43,580 --> 00:03:46,320
Nobody says "Stand", because the only people who

105
00:03:46,320 --> 00:03:47,920
know what that is think it's a joke.

106
00:03:49,040 --> 00:03:49,560
Lesson six:

107
00:03:50,260 --> 00:03:52,200
the ones who'd understand are the ones who'd

108
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:53,100
never believe it.

109
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:56,960
Fans love the scenes where Star Platinum catches

110
00:03:56,960 --> 00:03:57,320
bullets.

111
00:03:57,840 --> 00:04:00,580
So you ask how fast is fast enough.

112
00:04:01,240 --> 00:04:02,040
You don't test it.

113
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:02,840
You never would.

114
00:04:03,380 --> 00:04:04,440
You do it on a whiteboard.

115
00:04:05,160 --> 00:04:08,100
Human visual reaction time is about a quarter

116
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:10,497
of a second, between one hundred and ninety

117
00:04:10,497 --> 00:04:11,820
and two hundred and eighty-five milliseconds.

118
00:04:12,900 --> 00:04:15,240
A nine-millimetre pistol bullet leaves the barrel at

119
00:04:15,240 --> 00:04:17,540
around three hundred and fifty to four hundred

120
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:19,060
and ten metres per second.

121
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:22,120
Our own maths: at three hundred and sixty,

122
00:04:22,120 --> 00:04:25,080
it crosses ten metres in about twenty-eight milliseconds.

123
00:04:25,960 --> 00:04:28,360
That's about nine times shorter than your reaction

124
00:04:28,360 --> 00:04:28,780
time.

125
00:04:29,860 --> 00:04:32,280
Usain Bolt, the fastest human ever measured,

126
00:04:32,900 --> 00:04:34,540
averaged about ten metres per second.

127
00:04:35,120 --> 00:04:36,000
You put the marker down.

128
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:38,660
There's one thing in the story that doesn't

129
00:04:38,660 --> 00:04:39,900
depend on speed at all.

130
00:04:40,520 --> 00:04:42,500
Near the end of Part three, Jotaro learns

131
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:43,400
to stop time,

132
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:45,300
about five seconds at first.

133
00:04:45,980 --> 00:04:46,640
You don't have that.

134
00:04:47,400 --> 00:04:47,820
You think.

135
00:04:48,640 --> 00:04:51,940
Lesson seven: being fast doesn't make you fast

136
00:04:51,940 --> 00:04:52,760
enough for everything.

137
00:04:54,180 --> 00:04:56,800
By day thirty you can't stop catching things.

138
00:04:57,480 --> 00:04:57,480
Cups.

139
00:04:57,980 --> 00:04:58,320
Phones.

140
00:04:58,940 --> 00:04:59,680
A tray of glasses.

141
00:05:00,080 --> 00:05:01,440
A toddler falling off a wall.

142
00:05:02,240 --> 00:05:03,840
Your shoulders are always tight.

143
00:05:04,380 --> 00:05:06,060
You watch the edges of every table.

144
00:05:06,060 --> 00:05:08,580
Your friends notice you never relax.

145
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:10,880
Your hands shake every evening.

146
00:05:11,560 --> 00:05:13,820
Lesson eight: when you can catch everything,

147
00:05:14,440 --> 00:05:16,620
you start feeling responsible for everything.

148
00:05:18,120 --> 00:05:20,560
On day forty, a delivery van rolls down

149
00:05:20,560 --> 00:05:22,200
a hill with nobody in it.

150
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:24,980
At the bottom there's a school crossing, and

151
00:05:24,980 --> 00:05:27,040
the lollipop lady has just stepped out.

152
00:05:27,840 --> 00:05:28,260
You run.

153
00:05:28,860 --> 00:05:29,920
You're not going to make it.

154
00:05:30,340 --> 00:05:31,940
Star Platinum isn't going to make it either.

155
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:34,040
So you do the only thing you've never

156
00:05:34,040 --> 00:05:34,380
tried.

157
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:36,020
You hold your breath.

158
00:05:36,700 --> 00:05:37,520
The world goes silent.

159
00:05:38,100 --> 00:05:39,740
The van hangs in the air like a

160
00:05:39,740 --> 00:05:40,120
photograph.

161
00:05:40,780 --> 00:05:42,960
The birds stop mid-wing.

162
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:43,760
You reach the van.

163
00:05:44,560 --> 00:05:46,460
Star Platinum puts both hands on the bonnet

164
00:05:46,460 --> 00:05:47,800
and turns it toward a hedge.

165
00:05:48,540 --> 00:05:49,300
You step back.

166
00:05:50,080 --> 00:05:50,700
Your lungs burn.

167
00:05:51,380 --> 00:05:52,240
That's all you get:

168
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:53,480
one breath.

169
00:05:54,380 --> 00:05:55,340
And the world starts again.

170
00:05:56,040 --> 00:05:57,840
The van hits the hedge at walking speed.

171
00:05:58,660 --> 00:05:59,260
Nobody is hurt.

172
00:06:00,280 --> 00:06:02,840
The lollipop lady blinks at you, and keeps

173
00:06:02,840 --> 00:06:03,580
holding her sign.

174
00:06:04,940 --> 00:06:05,560
The next morning

175
00:06:06,220 --> 00:06:07,240
you can't lift your hands,

176
00:06:07,940 --> 00:06:09,840
and they don't stop shaking until lunch.

177
00:06:10,940 --> 00:06:12,740
Now you know what it really is.

178
00:06:13,620 --> 00:06:14,460
Not a superpower.

179
00:06:15,360 --> 00:06:17,680
A second body that never gets tired,

180
00:06:18,300 --> 00:06:19,440
attached to one that does.

181
00:06:20,800 --> 00:06:24,040
Lesson nine: the strongest thing about you is

182
00:06:24,040 --> 00:06:26,420
still the most tired thing about you.

183
00:06:27,200 --> 00:06:29,820
The next morning, seven twelve.

184
00:06:29,820 --> 00:06:31,800
A glass slides off your nightstand.

185
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:33,840
This time you reach first.

186
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:34,880
You miss.

187
00:06:35,580 --> 00:06:38,160
And something catches it anyway.

188
00:06:39,060 --> 00:06:40,880
So if you woke up tomorrow with Star

189
00:06:40,880 --> 00:06:41,320
Platinum,

190
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:43,900
would you keep it, or give it back?

191
00:06:44,880 --> 00:06:46,580
And what's the first thing you'd catch?

192
00:06:47,320 --> 00:06:47,940
Tell me in the comments.
